WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.500
Moving from SwiftUI to React Native requires mapping and architecture built on chained modifiers to one built on isolated objects.

2
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:14.900
The logic doesn't transfer one to one because the underlying execution model is different.

3
00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:21.900
While it uses CSS-like property names, there is no cascading style inheritance and no class selectors.

4
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:27.500
Each layout property is explicitly declared and every view relies on flexbox by default.

5
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:31.500
The framework also bypasses the sequential view modifier pattern.

6
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:36.700
You cannot chain design methods directly onto a component to build its appearance step by step.

7
00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:40.400
Styling instead relies on plain JavaScript object literals.

8
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:46.400
Every component receives its design instructions through a single explicit object containing key value pairs.

9
00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.000
These objects are injected into the component via the style prop.

10
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.600
Because there is no cascade, these definitions are isolated.

11
00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:57.900
A style applied to one node has no side effects on its siblings or children.

12
00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:03.700
Styling in this environment means mapping these isolated data objects directly to UI nodes,

13
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:07.600
a distinct departure from the fluid chaining found in SwiftUI.

14
00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:12.500
The engine that powers the static baseline styling is sheetstyle.create.

15
00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:15.900
It wraps your JavaScript objects in a dedicated API.

16
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:20.000
During development, passing objects through this method validates properties.

17
00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:24.200
It catches errors before rendering, providing immediate console feedback.

18
00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:26.600
At runtime, it's an optimization layer.

19
00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:32.000
The engine uses these structures to serialize styles and send them across the bridge to the native thread.

20
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.700
Static styles are only half the architecture.

21
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:37.600
Views must respond to dynamic state changes.

22
00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:44.400
Exactly like SwiftUI, react native components automatically re-render whenever they're underlying state variables update.

23
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:51.600
When styles depend on continuous numeric values like a scroll position coordinate or a progress bar width,

24
00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:58.200
computing those inline style objects directly inside the render cycle provides the necessary responsiveness.

25
00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.800
This introduces a structural challenge.

26
00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:07.000
How do you combine your static validated baseline styles with these conditional dynamic updates?

27
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:10.100
React native achieves composition through arrays.

28
00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:13.300
You pass an array of style objects to the style prop.

29
00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:20.400
The engine processes them sequentially, and the last item in the array overrides any identical properties to find earlier.

30
00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.300
To manage this, the framework provides stylesheet.flatten.

31
00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:29.600
It resolves these arrays, merging multiple overriding layers into a single object.

32
00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:34.000
This is necessary for introspective logic, like checking a background color,

33
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.400
or passing styles to external libraries that expect plain objects.

34
00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:44.500
Style sheet functions as the primary serialization engine, ensuring predictable property composition,

35
00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:47.700
and providing the bridge for performant native rendering.

36
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:54.200
In UI kit, the UI appearance API automatically manages global styling across the application.

37
00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:57.700
React native lacks a built-in equivalent for global appearance.

38
00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:00.600
The standard approach requires manual wiring.

39
00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:04.500
Define your colors and typography in a centralized theme module,

40
00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:08.300
wrapping your component tree in a React context provider umbrella.

41
00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:13.300
Child components extract this data by hooking into the context to read the global state.

42
00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:17.800
This manual environment routing extends to handling OS level user preferences,

43
00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:19.400
starting with dark mode.

44
00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:22.900
Where SwiftUI uses at environment dot color scheme,

45
00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:26.600
the direct react native equivalent is the use color scheme hook.

46
00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:30.900
For scalable applications, the best practice is to abstract use color scheme

47
00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.100
within your global theme context.

48
00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:37.600
This ensures individual components simply consume the active theme colors

49
00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:40.600
without calculating dark mode logic themselves.

50
00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:46.400
Typography requires a similar manual intervention when translating iOS dynamic type.

51
00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:50.300
React native text components read the system font scale preferences

52
00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:55.200
and adjust their size automatically, utilizing the allow font scaling prop.

53
00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:59.700
However, unlike iOS, the semantic layout containers holding that text

54
00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:03.000
do not automatically scale to match the increased font size,

55
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:06.200
which can lead to clipped text and broken UI boundaries.

56
00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:11.500
The manual workaround for scaling semantic layouts requires the pixel ratio API.

57
00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:16.200
You take your base integer size, multiply it by the pixel ratio font scale factor

58
00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:20.200
and apply the resulting value to expand the layout containers dynamically.

59
00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:23.700
Global styling and react native shifts the architectural burden,

60
00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:27.300
moving environment state out of the OS layer and directly into the developer's

61
00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:31.400
manual context design, colors and react native are handled plainly.

62
00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:37.300
There is no native color struct, everything from hex codes and RGBA values

63
00:04:37.300 --> 00:04:40.900
to CSS named colors is passed as a string.

64
00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:43.700
Custom fonts are similarly string based.

65
00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:48.700
While the native linkage process using info.plist mirrors iOS development,

66
00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:53.500
the string passed to the font family prop must match the internal registered name

67
00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:57.200
compiled into the native binary, not the local file name.

68
00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:00.300
The abstraction breaks down when rendering hardware shadows,

69
00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:04.200
as the framework must negotiate two different OS rendering engines.

70
00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:09.900
On iOS, shadows are calculated and applied using specific CAL layer properties.

71
00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:13.600
React native provides a one-to-one mapping for iOS devices,

72
00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:18.300
shadow color, shadow offset, shadow opacity and shadow radius.

73
00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:21.000
Android ignores these CAL layer properties.

74
00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:25.200
It relies strictly on material design's Z-axis elevation integer,

75
00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:28.600
which physically lifts the element to cast a programmatic shadow.

76
00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:32.700
Managing this divergence without creating a tangled mess of conditional logic

77
00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:35.000
requires platform dot select.

78
00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:40.600
This method accepts dictionary keys like iOS, Android and default

79
00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:45.900
and routes the correct platform specific style object to the render engine at runtime.

80
00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:50.700
Achieving native polish means writing specific definitions for these anomalies

81
00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:55.400
rather than relying on a universal, lowest common denominator abstraction.

82
00:05:55.400 --> 00:06:01.500
Developers migrating from web backgrounds often use libraries that mimic traditional web paradigms.

83
00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:04.500
Libraries like styled components slash native

84
00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:10.000
exist to provide identical API parity for teams porting React DOM projects.

85
00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:17.000
Similarly, native wind translates the utility class syntax of tailwind CSS into React native logic.

86
00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:22.400
Despite these alternatives, the native style sheet remains the standard for mobile applications,

87
00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:27.300
avoiding third-party dependency overhead while ensuring direct engine optimization.

88
00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:30.300
This split-screen comparison mapped the architecture.

89
00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:34.800
First, chained.modifier shifts to the explicit style prop.

90
00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:39.300
Next, UI appearance translates to a manual theme context,

91
00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:43.000
and at environment moves to the use color scheme hook.

92
00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:47.100
Finally, CAL layer routes via platform dot select

93
00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:52.000
and iOS dynamic type replicates using pixel ratio multipliers.

94
00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:56.100
Mastering React native styling means learning to explicitly serialize

95
00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:59.600
and route design data across the JavaScript to native bridge.

